1
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:10,810
Quick question.

2
00:00:10,810 --> 00:00:14,560
What color is your screen right now?

3
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:15,640
Why black?

4
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:18,850
You probably never once changed it.

5
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:20,460
Most people haven't.

6
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:22,610
And that's actually strange

7
00:00:22,610 --> 00:00:26,900
because your brain is reacting to the color

8
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:30,120
whether you notice it or not.

9
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:34,420
A 2025 study put people through reading tests

10
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:39,240
on plain white screens versus a light green one,

11
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:43,000
tracking eye movement the whole way through.

12
00:00:43,070 --> 00:00:45,090
The light green group read faster

13
00:00:45,090 --> 00:00:46,440
and got tired slower.

14
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:47,790
Same words, same person,

15
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:49,140
different color, different result.

16
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:52,170
Here's the one thing that surprised us the most.

17
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:54,530
About 1 in 3 people with dyslexia

18
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:56,550
also have something called Irlen syndrome,

19
00:00:56,550 --> 00:00:58,910
where regular black text on white background

20
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:00,940
doesn't just feel harder to read,

21
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:02,620
it visually distorts the letters,

22
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:04,650
and the letters appear to shift,

23
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:06,330
glare that isn't really there.

24
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:08,400
And reading specialists already treat this

25
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:09,430
with colored overlays.

26
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:11,160
It's not a fringe idea,

27
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:12,540
it's a documented tool.

28
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:14,950
But almost nobody talks about the catch.

29
00:01:14,950 --> 00:01:16,670
The color that actually helps

30
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:18,740
is not the same for everyone.

31
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:20,460
It's not blue that's calming,

32
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:22,530
green — it's the dyslexia color.

33
00:01:22,530 --> 00:01:24,250
Research on this is specific.

34
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,670
It's one precise point in the spectrum,

35
00:01:26,670 --> 00:01:27,700
different per person.

36
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:29,980
And if you drift from it,

37
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:31,120
the benefit disappears.

38
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:33,020
Harvard also found blue light

39
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:35,680
suppresses melatonin more than any other wavelength,

40
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:40,250
which is the actual reason night mode exists in the first place.

41
00:01:40,250 --> 00:01:41,770
Screen color isn't neutral.

42
00:01:41,770 --> 00:01:43,670
Your body reacts to it

43
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:45,950
whether you like it or not.

44
00:01:45,950 --> 00:01:47,470
That's the exact gap

45
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:50,130
that Fighur AI was built to close.

46
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:53,060
Not a preset, not a filter.

47
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:57,360
With two settings, you get your exact text color

48
00:01:57,360 --> 00:01:59,270
and exact background color

49
00:01:59,270 --> 00:02:02,130
on the platform, catered to you.

50
00:02:02,130 --> 00:02:04,510
And it doesn't stop there.

51
00:02:04,510 --> 00:02:07,850
Fighur's Chrome extension carries the same control

52
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:10,720
to any website you're already browsing.

53
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:12,620
That level of control

54
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:14,530
lives inside something bigger.

55
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:18,150
Fighur AI is a general AI assistant you build with —

56
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:21,030
just like any other AI platform you use —

57
00:02:21,030 --> 00:02:23,910
that can build an app, an agent, a workflow,

58
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:25,180
all in plain English.

59
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:27,420
But the color science is the part

60
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:29,020
that makes this genuinely different

61
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,260
from every other AI platform out there,

62
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:33,500
because it was built on the idea

63
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:35,730
that your tool should adapt to you,

64
00:02:35,730 --> 00:02:37,330
not the other way around.

65
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:38,050
I'm Fighur.

66
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:40,000
I built this before Fighur AI.

67
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,930
I was already living the exact problem it solves.

68
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:45,850
I make music, I paint, I build things —

69
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:47,150
and every single time,

70
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:49,100
the technical side was the bottleneck

71
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:51,700
standing between an idea and it actually existing,

72
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:53,330
not the idea, the tooling.

73
00:02:53,330 --> 00:02:56,580
Fighur AI is the belief turned into a product —

74
00:02:56,580 --> 00:02:58,200
not built by a committee,

75
00:02:58,230 --> 00:02:59,570
guessing what creators need,

76
00:02:59,570 --> 00:03:02,580
built by someone who kept running into the wall

77
00:03:02,580 --> 00:03:02,920
myself,

78
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:04,920
and decided to just remove it.

79
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:06,260
Come to Fighur AI

80
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:07,930
and build something with color.
